    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1079">

                <pb facs="0987" n="673" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg673"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/411</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-08-09T14:00:00+01:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 9, 1914, 2 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 14, 8:30 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Germany wants permission to send telegrams in cipher via Tuckerton wireless
                    station which is in connection with Eilvese station here in Hanover. I suggest
                    while waiting this permission, if you give it unconditionally, for the moment,
                    Germany allowing me to send you daily one long despatch in cipher or English and
                    that you be allowed to answer it in same manner. Send <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tennessee</hi>, Rotterdam. Germany wants to communicate their Embassy at
                    Tokyo. Better keep this communication your hands so we can keep this line open
                    via Tuckerton.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
